# The LIGHT gate: lint-staged FIXES eslint and prettier on the STAGED files and stages the fixes into
# this same commit, then `complete:light:gates`.
# Staged-only so a peer session's dirty file cannot fail, or be rewritten by, an unrelated commit;
# CI lints the whole tree. It keeps every static check (type-check, lint, both spec lints, format,
# the contract-bundle, docs and cross-repo checks) plus the unit and cross-cutting suites — nothing
# that starts `mongod`.
#
# Left out, apart from the staged files `test:staged` runs at the end (CI still runs all of it).
# When each gate runs: docs/build/testing-and-docs.md#when-each-gate-runs
#
# - `test:integration`, `test:contract` and `test:fuzz` — the database and HTTP layers, fuzz being
#   most of the full gate's minutes;
# - `docs:build`.
#
# The spec-identity check SKIPS rather than fails when the paired frontend is not on disk, so a
# half-cloned pair can still commit. It stays fatal under CI, where a missing sibling means the
# workflow is misconfigured.
#
# Deliberately not here, and run by hand for the occasions that want them: `test:mutation`
# (re-executes the unit suite once per mutant — nightly in CI), `complete:manual` (`test:prism`,
# which binds a real port), and `bench` (needs a running server, and reports numbers
# rather than a verdict).
# Write the generated artefacts first, so the gates below read them off disk. Costs about thirty
# seconds.
#
# `--no-sync` is not optional. A full `regenerate` ends with `sync:frontend`, which writes into the
# PAIRED REPO's working tree — a commit here would silently dirty a checkout you are not looking
# at, and possibly one with its own work in progress. Hand the files over deliberately, with
# `npm run regenerate` by itself, when you mean to.
npm run regenerate -- --no-sync

# lint-staged: `eslint --fix` then `prettier --write` on the staged files only, and `git add` of
# exactly the files those fixers changed. Its own backup stash hides the unstaged hunks of a
# partially staged file while the fixers run, and puts them back unstaged afterwards; in a `git commit`
# that concludes a merge it touches only the files that differ from both parents (it finds them
# through MERGE_HEAD, which does not exist yet when `pre-merge-commit` runs). A file `regenerate` rewrote above
# is not one the fixers changed, so it is never staged here. `.lintstagedrc.json` holds the tasks.
# https://github.com/lint-staged/lint-staged#how-does-lint-stageds-stashing-work
npx lint-staged

# Check that what is being COMMITTED — the index, after the fixes above — is current. A regenerated
# file you did not stage is not in the commit, and this refuses the commit for it instead of
# papering over it. Costs about thirty seconds.
#
# What stays committed and is therefore checked: `shared/rules/schemas/*.json`,
# `shared/authorization-keys.generated.yaml`, `docker/crontab` and the
# tables inside `docs/`. Everything else `regenerate` writes (`openapi.yaml`, both asyncapi
# bundles, `api.generated/`, every `*.generated.ts`) is gitignored: postinstall rebuilds it, and the gates
# below read it off disk.
#
# `check:generated` exports the index (`git checkout-index`, so a partial `git commit -- <paths>`
# is honoured) to a scratch folder, regenerates there and fails if anything changed. It names the
# files; the fix is `npm run regenerate` and staging them.
npm run check:generated
npm run complete:light:gates

# Last, because it is the one step that starts `mongod`: every integration, contract or fuzz test
# file this commit adds or changes, run by file. Light skips those layers, so a new one would
# otherwise first run at merge time. See scripts/testing/run-staged-tests.ts.
npm run test:staged
